## Agenda
1. As a group, go through the proposed build approach (source code → understand → Java UI).
2. Make sure everyone is clear on the plan before it is finalized.
3. Go over the tools being used for planning and communication.

## Discussion
- The team leader guided the group through the concept of beginning with real STC89C52 source code instead of relying only on the datasheet.
- The team talked about passing that source code through a compiler so register/peripheral behavior could be observed directly, before recreating the same behavior in Java.
- The discussion also covered linking the Java simulation logic to a UI so the behavior can be visualized, rather than being run only on hardware.
- The tools currently being used for the project were reviewed:
  - **Miro** — shared board for planning and task breakdown (used in Meeting 1).
  - **Google Chat** — day-to-day coordination among members.
  - **Google Meet with transcription** — used to record and transcribe meetings for documentation.
- Some earlier communication friction was mentioned (a member was hard to reach around Meeting 1); this was addressed by moving primary coordination to Google Chat going forward.

## Decisions
1. The build approach was finalized: compile and run reference source code to understand real behavior, then implement equivalent behavior in Java with a connected UI. (See `docs/decisions/decision-log.md`, Decision 004.)
2. Miro will continue to be used for planning, Google Chat for day-to-day coordination, and Google Meet (with transcription) for meetings — all official decisions still get written up in `docs/` per project rules, not left only in chat/meeting transcripts.

## Conclusion
1. We understood the plan and recorded it in files, which we now upload accordingly to github.
